		<div class="story-heads">
		<h1>What Should You Read Next? Here Are the Best Reviewed Books of the Week</h1>
		<h2>Featuring Anjet Daanje, Carlos the Jackal, Dorthe Nors, and More</h2>	</div>
	</div>
										<div class="byline-date">
					<span class="byline"><a href="https://lithub.com/author/book-marks/"  title="Book Marks">Book Marks</a></span>
					<span class="hr"></span>
					<span class="date">August 21, 2026</span>
				</div>
						<div class="story-body">
								<p>Anjet Daanje’s <em>The Song of Stork and Dromedary</em>, Joby Warrick’s <em>The Jackal</em>, and Dorthe Nors’ <em>Range</em> all feature among the best reviewed books of the week.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Brought to you by <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book Marks</a>, Lit Hub’s home for book reviews.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">*</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/category/fiction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Fiction</strong></a></p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-136814 aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/affe2a84ffd4bf517694acdc70781425-195x300.gif" sizes="(max-width: 195px) 100vw, 195px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/affe2a84ffd4bf517694acdc70781425-195x300.gif 195w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/affe2a84ffd4bf517694acdc70781425-33x50.gif 33w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/affe2a84ffd4bf517694acdc70781425.gif 422w" alt="The Song of Stork and Dromedary" width="195" height="300" data-attachment-id="136814" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/the-song-of-stork-and-dromedary/the-song-of-stork-and-dromedary-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/affe2a84ffd4bf517694acdc70781425.gif" data-orig-size="422,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="The Song of Stork and Dromedary Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/affe2a84ffd4bf517694acdc70781425.gif" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>1. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-song-of-stork-and-dromedary/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Song of Stork and Dromedary</em></a> by Anjet Daanje<br />
(FSG)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>4 Rave • 1 Positive</strong></p>
<p>“Daanje’s variations never become schematic or predictable. The novel’s length is daunting, but the writing is swift and eventful, often expressed in coursing run-on sentences. The reader advances across Mr. McKay’s superb translation as though compelled by a fierce highland wind. In the book’s telling, the Drayden sisters’ imaginative visions are so powerful that they reshape the way people experience reality. Readers, too, will finish this vast, teeming novel with a sense of a world transformed.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Sam Sacks (<a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-song-of-stork-and-dromedary/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Wall Street Journal</em></a>)</p>
<p><a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-maltese-version/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" class="wp-image-136812 size-medium aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/71a51f52fabc9da249a987836af5c518-194x300.gif" sizes="(max-width: 194px) 100vw, 194px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/71a51f52fabc9da249a987836af5c518-194x300.gif 194w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/71a51f52fabc9da249a987836af5c518-32x50.gif 32w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/71a51f52fabc9da249a987836af5c518.gif 419w" alt="The Maltese Version" width="194" height="300" data-attachment-id="136812" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/the-maltese-version/the-maltese-version-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/71a51f52fabc9da249a987836af5c518.gif" data-orig-size="419,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="The Maltese Version Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/71a51f52fabc9da249a987836af5c518.gif" /></a></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>2. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-maltese-version/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Maltese Version</em></a> by Katy Simpson Smith<br />
(FSG)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>3 Rave • 2 Positive<br />
Read an excerpt from <em>The Maltese Version</em> <a href="https://lithub.com/the-maltese-version/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a></strong></p>
<p>“Philosophical and disarmingly dishy … The meringue-light surface of <em>The Maltese Version,</em> which had thus far presented itself as a caper about two female friends trying to make it in the dying travel-guide industry, might conceal unforeseen depths … Smith is an observant and dexterous writer … A steely exploration of novelistic form and what said form can and cannot reveal. It’s a book that—for both its inventiveness and its many linguistic sleights of hand—can renew our faith in the playful joys and critical capacity of fiction.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Lucy Ives (<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/16/books/review/the-maltese-version-katy-simpson-smith.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="external"><em>The New York Times Book Review</em></a>)</p>
<p><a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-book-of-chuck/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" class="wp-image-136809 size-medium aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/87fe5fca9d573906ea101ec4b7f90752-199x300.gif" sizes="(max-width: 199px) 100vw, 199px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/87fe5fca9d573906ea101ec4b7f90752-199x300.gif 199w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/87fe5fca9d573906ea101ec4b7f90752-33x50.gif 33w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/87fe5fca9d573906ea101ec4b7f90752.gif 429w" alt="The Book of Chuck" width="199" height="300" data-attachment-id="136809" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/the-book-of-chuck/the-book-of-chuck-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/87fe5fca9d573906ea101ec4b7f90752.gif" data-orig-size="429,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="The Book of Chuck Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/87fe5fca9d573906ea101ec4b7f90752.gif" /></a></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>3. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-book-of-chuck/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Book of Chuck</em></a> by Latoya Watkins<br />
(Tiny Reparations Books)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>4 Rave</strong></p>
<p>“Lyrical … Feels like a poetic allegory for both generational trauma and generational healing … We may yearn for the acceptance of others while neglecting to spare enough respect for ourselves. The story of Chuck’s troubled family suggests there is room for that self-regard to blossom.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Charmaine Wilkerson (<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/18/books/review/the-book-of-chuck-latoya-watkins.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="external"><em>The New York Times Book Review</em></a>)</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-136807 aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/ab3f4c9c20f28665226ddde3bd796833-200x300.gif" sizes="auto, (max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/ab3f4c9c20f28665226ddde3bd796833-200x300.gif 200w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/ab3f4c9c20f28665226ddde3bd796833-33x50.gif 33w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/ab3f4c9c20f28665226ddde3bd796833.gif 432w" alt="Range Cover" width="200" height="300" data-attachment-id="136807" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/range/range-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/ab3f4c9c20f28665226ddde3bd796833.gif" data-orig-size="432,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="Range Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/ab3f4c9c20f28665226ddde3bd796833.gif" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>4. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/range/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Range</em></a> by Dorthe Nors<br />
(Graywolf)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>2 Rave • 3 Positive<br />
Read an excerpt from <em>Range</em> <a href="https://lithub.com/range/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a></strong></p>
<p>“In an era when all language is subject to relentless algorithmic flattening, when books are so often rewarded for easy comprehension and bland sentiment, <em>Range</em> is a rebellious celebration of the singularity, and mystery, of humankind. We need more fiction like Nors’s. Let the weird children come.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Charlotte Wood (<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/18/books/review/range-dorthe-nors.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="external"><em>The New York Times Book Review</em></a>)</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-136826 aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/b08272a28bb023568ca0db3daf442be3-195x300.gif" sizes="auto, (max-width: 195px) 100vw, 195px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/b08272a28bb023568ca0db3daf442be3-195x300.gif 195w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/b08272a28bb023568ca0db3daf442be3-33x50.gif 33w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/b08272a28bb023568ca0db3daf442be3.gif 422w" alt="Unprecedented Times" width="195" height="300" data-attachment-id="136826" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/unprecedented-times/unprecedented-times-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/b08272a28bb023568ca0db3daf442be3.gif" data-orig-size="422,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="Unprecedented Times Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/b08272a28bb023568ca0db3daf442be3.gif" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>5. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/unprecedented-times/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Unprecedented Times</em></a> by Malavika Kannan<br />
(Henry Holt)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>2 Rave • 1 Positive<br />
Read an excerpt from <em>Unprecedented Times</em> <a href="https://lithub.com/unprecedented-times/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a></strong></p>
<p>“Rishi’s relationship with her mother and how it evolves over the course of the novel was one of the most moving parts of the book … Another impressive feat of <em>Unprecedented Times</em>: Kannan’s ability to manage so many characters while developing them all sufficiently to feel vivid and real … The ending has lingered in my mind long after the final page. Her story is compelling because of her contradictions and how we watch them play out over the course of the novel.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Mia Arias Tsang (<a href="https://www.autostraddle.com/first-queer-love-gay-hookups-and-mother-daughter-tensions-abound-in-early-pandemic-set-unprecedented-times/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="external"><em>Autostraddle</em></a>)</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>**</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/category/non-fiction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Nonfiction</strong></a></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-136828 aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/c5120bbaa7529d3e752a132b19fc1d9b-200x300.gif" sizes="auto, (max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/c5120bbaa7529d3e752a132b19fc1d9b-200x300.gif 200w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/c5120bbaa7529d3e752a132b19fc1d9b-33x50.gif 33w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/c5120bbaa7529d3e752a132b19fc1d9b.gif 432w" alt="Togetherness" width="200" height="300" data-attachment-id="136828" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/togetherness-symbiosis-and-the-hidden-story-of-lifes-greatest-collaborations/togetherness-symbiosis-and-the-hidden-story-of-lifes-greatest-collaborations-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/c5120bbaa7529d3e752a132b19fc1d9b.gif" data-orig-size="432,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="Togetherness: Symbiosis and the Hidden Story of Life&amp;#8217;s Greatest Collaborations Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/c5120bbaa7529d3e752a132b19fc1d9b.gif" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>1. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/togetherness-symbiosis-and-the-hidden-story-of-lifes-greatest-collaborations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Togetherness: Symbiosis and the Hidden Story of Life’s Greatest Collaborations</em></a> by Rowan Hooper</strong><br />
<strong>(Knopf)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>3 Rave • 1 Positive • 2 Mixed</strong></p>
<p>“Not an attempt to make evolution cuddlier and more palatable; rather, it is a corrective deeply informed by what we have learned since Darwin about how nature works. Written with immense charm and passion, and packed with eye-popping facts, it is also a paean to the wonders of nature and the value and urgency of preserving them.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Philip Ball (<a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jun/15/togetherness-by-rowan-hooper-review-a-stunning-portrait-of-cooperation-in-nature" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="external"><em>The Guardian</em></a>)</p>
<p><a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-jackal-the-rise-and-fall-of-carlos-the-worlds-first-super-terrorist/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="wp-image-136786 size-medium aligncenter" src="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/a3bd91fec0483abf15f01938c0f53027-198x300.gif" sizes="auto, (max-width: 198px) 100vw, 198px" srcset="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/a3bd91fec0483abf15f01938c0f53027-198x300.gif 198w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/a3bd91fec0483abf15f01938c0f53027-33x50.gif 33w, https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/a3bd91fec0483abf15f01938c0f53027.gif 427w" alt="The Jackal" width="198" height="300" data-attachment-id="136786" data-permalink="https://bookmarks.reviews/bookmark/the-jackal-the-rise-and-fall-of-carlos-the-worlds-first-super-terrorist/the-jackal-the-rise-and-fall-of-carlos-the-worlds-first-super-terrorist-cover/" data-orig-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/a3bd91fec0483abf15f01938c0f53027.gif" data-orig-size="427,648" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="The Jackal: The Rise and Fall of Carlos, the World&amp;#8217;s First Super-Terrorist Cover" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://s26162.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/a3bd91fec0483abf15f01938c0f53027.gif" /></a></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>2. <a href="https://bookmarks.reviews/reviews/the-jackal-the-rise-and-fall-of-carlos-the-worlds-first-super-terrorist/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Jackal: The Rise and Fall of Carlos, the World’s First Super-Terrorist</em></a> by Joby Warrick<br />
(Scribner)</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>3 Rave • 1 Positive</strong></p>
<p>“Authoritative and acutely readable … Warrick acknowledges his many predecessors. He persuasively notes, fighting through the cliché, that new information has come to light.”</p>
<p style="text-align: right;">–Dwight Garner (<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/10/books/review/joby-warrick-carlos-the-jackal.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="external"><em>The New York Times</em></a>)</p>
